      "writtenOn": "2014-03-03T06:39:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the past, we can create an unscoped trust because we do not specify \"roles\" parameter. But in this patch, we will raise 403 response if \"roles\" parameter is not specified. So, we can not create an unscoped trust anymore.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAVn//+tA\u003d",
      "revId": "2422a85b93730751a5f20da479f9542ef825ca65",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAVn//4V0\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/trust/controllers.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 139,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-03-03T17:11:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This doesn\u0027t check to make sure the role passed in actually exists, it just throws an error if nothing is provided in \u0027roles\u0027. The API spec says that *if* a project_id is supplied in the POST request there should be at least one role associated with it. This doesn\u0027t fix that since we are not checking for project_id, and this will break the case where we don\u0027t provide a project_id or roles, causing inconsistency with the API spec.\n\n\nThere is some logic that exists to take care of some of this: http://git.openstack.org/cgit/openstack/keystone/tree/keystone/trust/controllers.py#n105 and might be an appropriate seam for a fix.",
      "revId": "2422a85b93730751a5f20da479f9542ef825ca65",
